{"content":"🚨 **BLOCKING: the `@Skip` annotation is inert — the group still runs and will break test collection**

`package:test` only honors `@Skip` when attached to `test()`/`group()`/`main` — as metadata on a plain helper function it does nothing (verified with a minimal probe: an `@Skip`'d helper's group executed and failed). Run the suite on this branch and you can see it: the two `factory/teammate.yml leg runner` tests are listed as ✅ passing, not ⏭️ skipped — only `factory_stub_ref_test.dart`'s `skip:`-parameter test is actually skipped.

Consequences:

1. The documented skip does not exist — CI keeps executing the leg-runner contract against the in-tree submodule copy that no longer receives any calls (the stub now calls `dmtools-agentic-workflows@b93dc51`).
2. At exactly the follow-up this skip documents (dmtools-agents#591 retires the copy + submodule bump), the group body's `_read(_teammateFactoryPath)` runs at **test collection time** (group bodies execute even for skipped groups) and its `fail('missing expected file: …')` will error the whole suite — the moment the skip is supposed to protect is the moment the suite breaks.

Fix (keeps registration safe forever):

```dart
void _teammateFactoryContract() {
  // Re-home to the dmtools-agentic-workflows pin as a follow-up (#591).
  if (!File(_teammateFactoryPath).existsSync()) return;
  group('factory/teammate.yml leg runner', skip: 'pending dmtools-agents#591', () {
    final yaml = _read(_teammateFactoryPath);
    ...
```

(or guard the `_teammateFactoryContract()` call site in `main()` with the same `existsSync()` check). CI checks out submodules (`quality.yml` `submodules: true`), so the file exists today and the failure only fires after the bump — but this PR is what sets up that bump path.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1_inert_skip.md"}

{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the freeze-model doc comments in both test files now contradict the code**

This PR changes the contract that the library-level docs still define — the docs were left on the old model:

- `factory_stub_ref_test.dart:6-16` still says: *"`ai-teammate.yml` still calls the historical factory-teammate.yml inside the `agents` submodule pin (teammate migration is a follow-up) — there the old in-lockstep contract holds: uses-SHA == submodule gitlink == the factory_ref input."* The new `_teammateLockstepPin` (this hunk) implements the opposite: uses-SHA = factory-home pin (dmtools-agentic-workflows), `factory_ref` = submodule gitlink, and the two SHAs are now deliberately **different**.
- `machine_sm_watchdog_test.dart:11-19` still says: *"Only the teammate leg still calls the historical factory inside the agents submodule (its migration is a follow-up), so its factory contract groups keep reading the in-tree submodule copy"* — the leg now calls the AW home and that group is (meant to be) skipped, not kept.

These headers are the spec the next maintainer reads before touching a pin; right now each file carries two contradictory contracts. Please update both headers to the two-pin freeze model, e.g.:

```
/// - `ai-teammate.yml` calls the factory home (dmtools-agentic-workflows)
///   pinned to its own immutable SHA; the `agents` submodule gitlink rides
///   the factory_ref input as the ENGINE pin — the two pins are independent
